### **2. Technical Explanations**

#### **A. Jensen–Shannon Divergence (JSD)** 📉

The author mentions using "JSD heatmaps" to check for drift.

* **What it is:** A mathematical method to measure the **distance** between two probability distributions. It is similar to the "KS Test" but smoother and symmetric.
* **How it works:**
    * Imagine two histograms: one for Train Data, one for Test Data.
    * **JSD = 0:** The histograms overlap perfectly (The data is identical).
    * **JSD = 1:** The histograms don't touch at all (The data is completely different).
* **Why use it:** It tells you **"How dangerous is this column?"**
    * If `Income` has a JSD of 0.01, it is safe to use.
    * If `Income` has a JSD of 0.5, the Test set has a totally different income bracket than your Train set. Your model will likely fail on this column.



#### **B. BRFSS (Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System)** 🏥

* **What it is:** This is the **Gold Standard** real-world dataset. It is a massive annual telephone survey conducted by the CDC (US Government) collecting health data from 400,000+ Americans.
* **Why the author used it:** They used it as a "Lie Detector."
